<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lon Nol</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1913">1913</a>–<a href="/years/1985">1985</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lon Nol was a Cambodian general and politician who served as defence minister and prime minister under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norodom-sihanouk">Norodom Sihanouk</a>. On 18 March 1970, while Sihanouk was abroad, he and the national assembly deposed him, and Lon Nol proclaimed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-empire">Khmer</a> Republic, becoming its president in 1972. Backed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, his government fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-rouge">Khmer Rouge</a> and North Vietnamese until he fled in April 1975. He died in the United States.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lon-nol.jpg" alt="Lon Nol" width="320" height="503" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:LonNol.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Lon Nol was born in Prey Veng Province on 13 November 1913 to a family of mixed Khmer and Chinese descent. He was educated at the Lycée Chasseloup-Laubat in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ho-chi-minh-city">Saigon</a> and at the Cambodian Royal Military Academy. He entered the French colonial civil service in 1937 as a magistrate and by 1946 was governor of Kratie Province. He joined the army in 1952 and fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viet-minh">Viet Minh</a>. After independence his nationalist Khmer Renovation party became part of the core of the Sangkum, the movement set up by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norodom-sihanouk">Norodom Sihanouk</a> for the elections of 1955.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>He was appointed army chief of staff in 1955, commander-in-chief in 1960 and deputy prime minister in 1963. He became prime minister after the elections of 1966, and the following year troops savagely repressed the Samlaut Uprising in Battambang Province. Injured in a car crash in 1967, he withdrew for a time, then returned as prime minister in 1969 with Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sisowath-sirik-matak">Sisowath Sirik Matak</a> as his deputy.</p><p>On 12 March 1970 the two men closed the port of Sihanoukville to the North Vietnamese and demanded that all North Vietnamese and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viet-cong">Viet Cong</a> forces leave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodia">Cambodia</a> within 72 hours. Lon Nol at first refused to see Sihanouk deposed; on the night of 17 March Sirik Matak, with three army officers, compelled him at gunpoint to sign the necessary documents. Pro-Sihanouk demonstrations on 28 and 29 March were suppressed with several hundred deaths. The republic was declared on 9 October 1970. Sihanouk later claimed that the CIA had planned the coup, but there is no concrete evidence of its involvement.</p><p>The regime carried out mass killings of Vietnamese Cambodians. After a stroke in February 1971 Lon Nol's rule grew erratic and authoritarian: he made himself Marshal in April and suspended the National Assembly in October. By 1975 his government held little more than <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phnom-penh">Phnom Penh</a> and the temple of Preah Vihear.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>He resigned on 1 April 1975, his name being first on a list of people the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-rouge">Khmer Rouge</a> had vowed to execute, and went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a> and then to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hawaii">Hawaii</a>. In 1979 he settled in Fullerton, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/california">California</a>, where he died of a heart condition on 17 November 1985. His brother Lon Non, a minister of the republic, was executed by the Khmer Rouge after the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phnom-penh">Phnom Penh</a>. Lon Nol called his ideology, a blend of nationalism and mysticism, Neo-Khmerism, and hoped to unite the Khmers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodia">Cambodia</a> with those of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mekong-delta">Mekong Delta</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thailand">Thailand</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lon Nol” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>On 18 March 1970 Cambodia's national assembly, led by Prime Minister <b>Lon Nol</b> and Prince Sisowath Sirik Matak, voted to remove Norodom Sihanouk as head of state while he was abroad.</blockquote><a href="/state/khmer-republic">Khmer Republic</a></li><li><blockquote>On 18 March 1970, while Sihanouk was abroad, the national assembly deposed him, and <b>Lon Nol</b> proclaimed the Khmer Republic.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-cambodia-1953-1970">Kingdom of Cambodia (1953-1970)</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Pou Rieng District, in what is now <a href="/countries/cambodia">Cambodia</a>. Died in Fullerton, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=11.55,105.26&amp;year=1959" aria-label="Pou Rieng District on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7446 1733 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Lon Nol"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7896 2003h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7896 2003h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=11.55,105.26&amp;year=1959">What was here in 1959? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
